p23/all: FAIL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

WHAT EACH OF THE THREE STAGES ACTUALLY SHOWS

1. ROOT: KILLED, not failed.  `589091 Killed` is SIGKILL from the OOM killer.
   It had enumerated all 3,414,729 base states down to the 343,896 live ones and
   regenerated 200 cubes, with mismatches=0 on every one of them.  Nothing
   contradicted the recorded root; the process simply died.

2. COVERAGE: UNDECIDED, reported as SAT.  cover_check.py classified the solver's
   result as `if 'UNSATISFIABLE' not in r.stdout: RESULT SAT`, never looking at
   CaDiCaL's exit code (10 = SAT, 20 = UNSAT, anything else = did not decide).
   So an OOM kill, a scheduler kill or a full disk all print the alarming
   `*** DOES NOT COVER -- BUG`.  The tells that this is what happened:
     - time=1840.00s, exactly round, which a real solve is not (p19's was 274.53s)
     - the OOM killer had already taken the root stage in the same allocation
     - the streaming path prints no witness assignment, so there is no model
     - the coverage CNF is 0.78 GiB and its LRAT proof is ~2 GiB
   FIXED 2026-09-12 in cover_check.py: three outcomes now, and an undecided run
   prints `RESULT UNKNOWN ... exit=N  *** NOT a verdict on coverage` and returns 3.
   Tested on all four shapes: real UNSAT, real SAT (--drop control), exit 137 with
   no output, and a silent exit 0.

3. CERT: HALF DERIVED, HALF RECORDED.  certroot.py hashes the coverage CNF from
   disk, so split_cover_cnf=4876f077... IS a real recomputation, and it matched
   the committed value -- that half of the p23 certificate did reproduce here.
   But --root-cnf is read from instances/p23.conf, so search_root_cnf was the
   RECORDED number, the root stage having been killed at cube 200.  "CERT
   (portable) matches the recorded value" therefore means only that two numbers,
   one of them copied from the conf, were re-hashed.  It is not a reproduction of
   the search half.
   FIXED 2026-09-12 in run.sh: `all` now stops at the first failed stage, and
   stage_cert states plainly when search_root_cnf was read from the conf rather
   than derived in the same run.

WHAT TO RERUN, AND HOW BIG

  The stages are single-threaded; the cores below are bought for their MEMORY,
  which on cpu_p1 is roughly 4 GB per allocated core.

    salloc --account=lia@cpu --partition=cpu_p1 --qos=qos_cpu-t3 \
           --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 --cpus-per-task=20 --hint=nomultithread --time=06:00:00
    KInduceDFS/jz_reproduce/run.sh p23 all

  Confirm it is a COMPUTE node, not a login node: a login node's memory cap will
  SIGKILL the root stage every time.  $JOBSCRATCH needs about 4 GB free for the
  0.78 GiB CNF plus its ~2 GiB proof.  Expect roughly 50 min for the root stage's
  base-state enumeration before the first cube, then the cube regeneration, then
  a coverage solve that took just under 20 minutes on one laptop core and should
  be taken as 1-3x that here.
